  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 245 PM Thursday... \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Shallow and isolated  convection brings negligible \nrain amounts this afternoon, with shower and storm chances and \ncoverage increasing tonight.\n\nMid-lvl water vapor imagery shows a void in synoptic forcing over \nthe Mid-Atlantic into the Carolinas as a pocket of dry air continues \nto overspread the area. Surface dew points in the mid-60s is fueling \naround 1000 J/kg of MLCAPE, 50-100 of which in the lowest 3 km, and \nsupporting isolated, infrequent, low-topped showers producing mostly \nsprinkles. These showers will likely continue for the next several \nhours before collapsing with the loss of diurnal heating and cooling \nboundary layer takes over. \n\nCoverage is expected to increase across the west this evening and \nespecially overnight into Fri morning. This will be driven by strong \nupper divergence overspreading the southern Appalachians and \nCarolinas as an open wave over the Southern Plains merges with a \nstrong northern stream disturbance diving south through the Upper \nGreat Lakes and reaching the OH and TN Valley's by 12z Fri. Hi-res \nguidance continues to highlight mostly consolidated waves of mostly \nlight showers rippling northeast from Upstate and Midlands of SC \ninto the NC Piedmont overnight. Isolated deeper cells appear \npossible with the increasing mid/upper-lvl support and could result \nin a few rumbles of thunder overnight.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Showers and thunderstorms will continue Friday \nwithin a pre-frontal moist axis over central NC, with a risk of a \nfew severe storms in the Sandhills and Coastal Plain midday through \nafternoon.\n\nCoverage and intensity is expected to further increase within the \nband of showers ongoing Fri morning as greater synoptic forcing \noverspread central portions of the forecast area. Widespread cloud \ncover Fri morning will greatly limit the release of surface-based \ninstability and ongoing showers will likely be elevated in nature \nthrough a majority of the morning hours. \n\nCloud cover erosion east of the band of moderate showers and \nisolated thunder should allow for surface temperatures to rise into \nthe mid/upper 70s with dew points in the mid/upper 60s to support \n500-1500 J/kg of MLCAPE by early afternoon. Latest guidance \ncontinues to highlight the I-95 corridor eastward into eastern NC as \nthe most likely area to realize this instability and support an area \nof deeper, surface-based convection. Enhanced southwesterly of 35-45 \nkts, gradual veering wind profile and increasing winds with height \nwill support gradually curved hodographs and drive storm \norganization into organized multicells and perhaps transient \nsupercells (most probable in areas of line breaks in a bookend \nvortex of a bowing segment). \n\nLocalized damaging wind gusts will be possible within the strongest \norganized storms, but a brief short-track tornado can't be ruled out \nfrom transient supercells within line breaks, although the tornado \nthreat remains low without any localized storm interactions \nmodifying the background environment since 0-1 SRH should mostly \nrange from 50-100 m2/s2. Most probable timing for severe weather \nwill be from 11 AM until around 5 PM along and east of the I-95 \ncorridor and perhaps as far west as Wake County.",
